size and how a home is run move its income far more than a city average shows. For a range on one Myrtle Beach address, run it through the Airbnb calculator . Profit Is an Airbnb in Myrtle Beach Profitable? Yes, when it's run well. The average Myrtle Beach Airbnb took in $25,807 in the 12 months to September 2026, and run by its owner at everyday costs, about $1,330 a month of that is left after running costs, so a home that costs less than that to own or rent makes money. A listing in the top quarter leaves about $2,310 a month . Cleaning it yourself keeps the $3,300 a year guests pay in cleaning fees, for about $1,610 a month. Handing the listing to a co-host at about 10% of nightly bookings still leaves about $1,140 a month. These amounts come before housing costs. Where the mortgage or rent is less each month, the difference is profit; a home owned outright keeps it all after property tax, home insurance and any HOA or condo fees. Myrtle Beach Airbnb Income, Expenses and Profit per Year *]:border-b-0"> A year of income, running costs and what is left for housing costs for an average and a top-quarter Myrtle Beach Airbnb Line Average listing Top-quarter listing Money in Nightly bookings $22,507 $38,600 Cleaning fees guests pay 13% of Myrtle Beach bookings $3,300 $5,650 Gross bookings $25,807 $44,250 Running costs Airbnb's fee About 14%: 15.5% for the 86% of Myrtle Beach listings on Simplified Pricing, 3% for the rest −$3,560 −$6,100 Cleaning and laundry Paid for by the cleaning fee; $0 if you clean yourself −$3,300 −$5,650 Utilities and internet About 5% of bookings −$1,290 −$2,210 Guest supplies Toiletries, coffee, paper goods: about 2% −$520 −$880 Repairs and upkeep Including linens and small replacements: about 3% −$770 −$1,330 Short-term rental insurance One policy, on top of Airbnb's free AirCover −$260 −$260 Permit and registration Where one is required: see Myrtle Beach's Airbnb rules −$130 −$130 Management $0 when you run it; co-hosts charge from about 10